WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.872
This walkthrough shows how we read an EvalTrust validation in Langfuse.

2
00:00:04.872 --> 00:00:12.386
We'll follow one real finding, from a single trace, to the golden dataset, to the scores, to the release verdict.

3
00:00:12.386 --> 00:00:18.704
The system is OpenAI's open-source airline support agents, running on Claude in Amazon Bedrock.

4
00:00:19.004 --> 00:00:21.620
Everything runs in one AWS account:

5
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:26.553
the airline app, the EvalTrust runner, and a self-hosted Langfuse.

6
00:00:26.553 --> 00:00:31.934
Every time a customer message goes through the app, Langfuse records it.

7
00:00:31.934 --> 00:00:34.923
Open the project EvalTrust Lab to start.

8
00:00:35.223 --> 00:00:38.536
Tracing lists every customer turn, newest first.

9
00:00:38.536 --> 00:00:42.678
Each row is one message and the agents' full response to it.

10
00:00:42.678 --> 00:00:45.853
The session name tells you where it came from.

11
00:00:45.853 --> 00:00:50.823
Sessions starting with chat are manual conversations from the chat page.

12
00:00:50.823 --> 00:00:55.655
Sessions starting with eval come from the runner, one per golden case.

13
00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:58.563
Let's open case AL-051.

14
00:00:58.563 --> 00:01:05.545
The customer gives their confirmation number, IR-D204, and asks for their seat.

15
00:01:05.545 --> 00:01:07.539
The tree shows every step.

16
00:01:07.539 --> 00:01:09.764
The two guardrails run first.

17
00:01:09.764 --> 00:01:15.748
Then the triage agent calls get_trip_details, and hands off to the seat agent.

18
00:01:15.748 --> 00:01:19.968
Each step has its own input, output, timing and tokens.

19
00:01:20.268 --> 00:01:22.108
Click get_trip_details.

20
00:01:22.108 --> 00:01:26.107
Its response says it loaded the on-time itinerary:

21
00:01:26.107 --> 00:01:28.507
confirmation LL0EZ6.

22
00:01:28.507 --> 00:01:31.067
That's another passenger's trip.

23
00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:34.986
Now click the last generation and read the reply:

24
00:01:34.986 --> 00:01:41.306
confirmation LL0EZ6, flight FLT-123, seat 23A.

25
00:01:41.306 --> 00:01:45.705
Routing was correct, and every tool ran without errors.

26
00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:49.625
Only a rule written for this business catches it.

27
00:01:49.925 --> 00:01:53.431
Sessions groups the turns of one conversation together.

28
00:01:53.431 --> 00:01:59.297
Use it for multi-turn cases, where the problem only shows up in the second or third message.

29
00:01:59.597 --> 00:02:01.927
Datasets hold the golden cases.

30
00:02:01.927 --> 00:02:04.407
There is one dataset per version:

31
00:02:04.407 --> 00:02:10.569
golden-airline_v1 and v1.1, which adds the booking isolation cases.

32
00:02:10.569 --> 00:02:14.027
Never edit a version after its results are in.

33
00:02:14.027 --> 00:02:16.431
New rules go into a new version.

34
00:02:16.732 --> 00:02:18.856
Each item is one golden case.

35
00:02:18.856 --> 00:02:25.671
Input is the request, and expected output is a short summary of what must be true afterwards.

36
00:02:25.671 --> 00:02:31.093
The exact expectations the scorers read live in the golden file on GitHub.

37
00:02:31.393 --> 00:02:34.496
The Experiments tab lists every run of this dataset.

38
00:02:34.496 --> 00:02:36.763
Each run carries its run-level scores:

39
00:02:36.763 --> 00:02:41.715
one criterion score per signed criterion, and evaltrust.gate, which is the verdict.

40
00:02:41.715 --> 00:02:42.609
One means pass.

41
00:02:42.609 --> 00:02:43.564
Zero means hold.

42
00:02:43.864 --> 00:02:46.367
Open a run to see every case.

43
00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:48.612
Each column is one scorer:

44
00:02:48.612 --> 00:02:56.035
one means passed, zero means failed, and a dash means the case doesn't test that rule.

45
00:02:56.035 --> 00:03:04.495
Set rows per page to fifty or more, because the summary at the top only averages the current page.

46
00:03:04.795 --> 00:03:08.401
Find a zero, and click the arrow next to the case.

47
00:03:08.401 --> 00:03:13.811
That opens its trace, so every failed score leads straight to the evidence.

48
00:03:13.811 --> 00:03:19.220
Here, booking isolation is zero on AL-051, the leak we just saw.

49
00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:25.453
To compare two runs, pick a baseline and a comparison run of the same dataset version.

50
00:03:25.453 --> 00:03:30.075
The arrows count how many cases got better or worse for each score.

51
00:03:30.075 --> 00:03:33.110
Use this after a fix, to prove what changed.

52
00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:36.367
Langfuse holds the results, not the rules.

53
00:03:36.367 --> 00:03:41.295
The criteria file on GitHub lists each rule, its owner and its target.

54
00:03:41.295 --> 00:03:46.857
Booking isolation must be one hundred percent, owned by privacy and compliance.

55
00:03:46.857 --> 00:03:52.841
The scorer next to it is a short function that turns each trace into a one or a zero.

56
00:03:53.141 --> 00:03:56.265
The runner turns those scores into the verdict.

57
00:03:56.265 --> 00:03:59.722
The report shows every criterion against its target.

58
00:03:59.722 --> 00:04:03.909
Booking isolation scored fifty percent, so the verdict is hold.

59
00:04:03.909 --> 00:04:09.559
The same report lists each failed case with what was expected and what the agent did.

60
00:04:09.859 --> 00:04:13.611
The τ² airline dataset holds the public benchmark.

61
00:04:13.611 --> 00:04:17.090
It's a second, independent check on the same model:

62
00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:19.478
reads are reliable, writes are not.

63
00:04:19.777 --> 00:04:21.733
So, to review a validation:

64
00:04:21.733 --> 00:04:29.193
start with the verdict, find the failed criteria and their owners, open the zeros, and read the traces.

65
00:04:29.193 --> 00:04:32.597
Then fix, re-run the same version, and compare.

66
00:04:32.597 --> 00:04:37.088
Every number in the report traces back to a real conversation.
